WHAT YOU ALREADY KNOW   What you already know
Chapter 3 established that a bond's price moves inversely with the market's required return, and quoted a five-year 6 per cent bond at a little under $960 when the market requires 7 per cent. This chapter computes that figure, and every figure like it.
4.1  Valuing a bond by discounting
A five-year bond with a par value of $1,000 and a 6 per cent annual coupon promises $60 at the end of each of five years and $1,000 at the end of the fifth. Its value is what those promises are worth today at the rate the market requires.
Two present-value tools do all the work. The annuity factor converts a stream of equal amounts into a single present value; at 7 per cent for five years it is 4.1002. The present-value factor converts a single future amount; at 7 per cent for five years it is 0.7130.
So at a required return of 7 per cent: the coupons are worth 60 × 4.1002 = 246.01, the principal is worth 1,000 × 0.7130 = 713.00, and the bond is worth 246.01 + 713.00 = 959.01. That is the "a little under $960" of Chapter 3, and the $41 shortfall against par is the market's charge for a coupon one point below what it now requires.
At a required return of 5 per cent the same two steps give a different answer: the annuity factor is 4.3295 and the present-value factor 0.7835, so the coupons are worth 60 × 4.3295 = 259.77, the principal 1,000 × 0.7835 = 783.50, and the bond 259.77 + 783.50 = 1,043.27. The bond trades at a premium because its coupon is better than the market now offers.
Figure F604-02 sets out the computation at both rates.
Figure F604-02. One bond, two required returns
@table
Component | At 7 per cent | At 5 per cent
Annuity factor, five years | 4.1002 | 4.3295
Present value of the coupons | 246.01 | 259.77
Present-value factor, five years | 0.7130 | 0.7835
Present value of the principal | 713.00 | 783.50
Value of the bond | 959.01 | 1,043.27
@end
Three mechanical points are examined often.
Semi-annual coupons halve the payment and the rate and double the periods. A 6 per cent bond paying twice a year, valued at a required 7 per cent, is thirty dollars for ten periods discounted at 3.5 per cent.
The discount rate is the market's required return on this risk, not the coupon rate and not the company's own view of itself. For a corporate bond it is the government yield for that maturity plus a credit spread.
Price and yield move inversely, always, and the relationship is curved rather than straight. A fall in yields raises the price slightly more than an equal rise in yields lowers it, which is the property called convexity and is why duration, which assumes a straight line, is an approximation.
