Answers and explanations are at the end of the chapter.
4.2  Preferred stock, and the same arithmetic
Preferred stock pays a fixed dividend and normally has no maturity, so it is a perpetuity: a stream of equal payments that never stops. The present value of a perpetuity is the payment divided by the required return.
A preferred share paying $5.00 a year, where the market requires 8 per cent for that risk, is worth 5.00 ÷ 0.08 = 62.50. If the required return rises to 10 per cent the same share is worth 5.00 ÷ 0.10 = 50.00. There is no principal to come back, so the price carries the entire adjustment — which makes preferred stock more sensitive to rates than most bonds, a point section 4.3 will make precisely.
The features of preferred stock follow from its position between debt and equity, and Chapter 5 sets them out in full. Two matter here. A cumulative preferred share carries forward any dividend not paid, and nothing may go to the common shareholders until the arrears are cleared. A participating preferred share shares in profits beyond its fixed rate. Both features raise what a buyer will pay and lower the rate the issuer has to offer.
Note what the perpetuity formula assumes and what the exam does with it. It assumes the payment is fixed and continues for ever, so it suits preferred stock exactly, a long-dated bond approximately, and a common share not at all until a growth rate is built in. That is the subject of the next chapter.
Figure F604-03 compares the three instruments.
Figure F604-03. Three instruments, one method
@table
Instrument | Payment pattern | Valuation | Sensitivity to rates
Bond | Coupons, then principal | Annuity plus a lump sum | Moderate: the principal anchors it
Preferred stock | A fixed dividend for ever | Payment over required return | High: nothing anchors it
Common stock | A growing dividend for ever | Payment over the return less growth | Highest
@end
EXAM TRAP   Perpetuities and the rate
In a perpetuity the rate must be expressed as a decimal and must match the payment period. An annual dividend over a monthly rate is a figure twelve times too large.
Preferred stock has no maturity and therefore no yield to maturity. Its yield is simply dividend over price.
A cumulative preferred dividend in arrears is not a liability on the balance sheet; it is disclosed, and it blocks the common dividend.
