4.3  Duration
Everybody knows a long bond moves more than a short one when rates change. Duration says how much more, in a single number.
Macaulay duration is the weighted average time to receive the bond's cash flows, where each time is weighted by the present value of the payment received then. A zero-coupon bond pays once, so its duration equals its maturity. A coupon bond returns some money earlier, so its duration is always shorter than its maturity.
Modified duration converts that into a price sensitivity: it is the Macaulay duration divided by one plus the required return per period, and it estimates the percentage change in price for a one-point change in yield.
WORKED EXAMPLE   Worked example: the five-year bond, measured
Step 1. The weights. Discounting the five payments at 7 per cent gives present values of 56.07, 52.41, 48.98, 45.77 and 755.79, which add to the price of 959.01. Almost four-fifths of the bond's value sits in the final payment.
Step 2. Macaulay duration. Weighting each year by its share of the price gives a weighted average of about 4.45 years, against a maturity of five. The coupons pull it in.
Step 3. Modified duration. 4.45 ÷ 1.07 = 4.16, so a one-point rise in yields should take about 4.16 per cent off the price.
Step 4. In money. 4.16 per cent of 959.01 is about $39.90, so the bond would fall to roughly $919 if required returns went from 7 to 8 per cent.
Check. The estimate is slightly pessimistic, and knowing why is the mark. Duration assumes the price-yield line is straight; it is curved, so the true fall is a little smaller than duration predicts and the true rise on a fall in yields a little larger. The curvature is convexity, and it always works in the holder's favour. For a one-point move the difference is small; for a five-point move on a long bond it is large.
Figure F604-04 shows what moves duration.
Figure F604-04. What makes a bond more sensitive to rates
@table
Change | Effect on duration | Why
Longer maturity | Higher | Money comes back later
Lower coupon | Higher | Less value returns early
Zero coupon | Highest for its maturity | All value arrives at the end
Lower yield | Higher | Later payments are discounted less
A call provision | Lower | The bond may end early
@end
Duration is used for three things, and the exam names all three. It compares the rate sensitivity of two bonds in one number. It estimates the price effect of a stated rate move. And it allows a portfolio to be matched to a liability — an insurer with claims expected in seven years holds assets of duration seven, so that a rate move changes the value of both sides together. That last use is called immunisation and it is the reason duration exists.
