4.4  Issuing and refinancing
Now the issuer's side. A company deciding to issue debt faces four questions, and the exam asks them in combination: how much, for how long, on what terms, and through what route.
The route is a choice between a public issue, which is registered, underwritten by an investment bank, rated by an agency and sold to many holders, and a private placement with a small number of institutions, which is quicker and cheaper to arrange and carries tighter covenants and a slightly higher rate. Below a certain size the fixed costs of a public issue cannot be justified, which is why most mid-sized companies borrow from banks and institutions rather than from the bond market. Chapter 9 describes the machinery of both.
The refinancing decision is a calculation the exam sets often, and the structure never changes. A company with an existing bond at a high coupon can call it, pay the call premium, pay the costs of issuing a new bond, and save the interest difference for the remaining life.
WORKED EXAMPLE   Worked example: a refinancing that works
Step 1. The position. A company has $10,000,000 of bonds outstanding at 8 per cent with five years to run. It can issue new five-year bonds at 6 per cent. The existing bonds are callable at 103, so the call premium is 3 per cent of par, and issuing the new bonds costs $200,000.
Step 2. The saving. Two points on ten million: 10,000,000 × 0.02 = 200,000 a year before tax. At a 25 per cent tax rate the interest saved is worth 200,000 × 0.75 = 150,000 a year after tax, because the interest was deductible.
Step 3. The cost. The call premium is 10,000,000 × 0.03 = 300,000 and the issue costs are 200,000, so 300,000 + 200,000 = 500,000 before tax. Both are deductible, so the after-tax cost is 500,000 × 0.75 = 375,000.
Step 4. The decision. 375,000 ÷ 150,000 = 2.50, so the refinancing pays for itself in two and a half years against five years of remaining life. Discounted properly at the company's after-tax cost of debt the net present value is positive, and the refinancing should go ahead.
Check. Three things would reverse the conclusion and all three are examinable. If only two years remained, the saving would not cover the cost. If the new issue were at a floating rate, the 6 per cent would be today's rate and not a five-year commitment. And if the existing bond were not callable, there would be no decision to take at all — which is why Chapter 3 insisted that a call provision is paid for in the coupon from the first day.
Figure F604-05 sets the calculation out as a template.
Figure F604-05. The refinancing template
@table
Line | This case | Note
Annual interest saved, before tax | 200,000 | The rate difference on the principal
Annual interest saved, after tax | 150,000 | Interest is deductible, so the saving is taxed
Call premium | 300,000 | A percentage of par, stated in the indenture
Issue costs of the new bonds | 200,000 | Underwriting, legal, rating
Total cost after tax | 375,000 | Both costs are deductible
Payback | 2.50 years | Against five years of remaining life
@end
