4.5  Taxes and the financing decision
Interest is deductible and dividends are not. That single asymmetry shapes capital structure everywhere, and the exam tests it in three forms.
The after-tax cost of debt. A company borrowing at 6 per cent with a 25 per cent tax rate bears 6.0 × 0.75 = 4.5 per cent, because each dollar of interest reduces taxable income by a dollar. Orontes's $390,000 of interest is worth 390,000 × 0.25 = 97,500 a year in tax saved, and that saving is called the interest tax shield.
Debt against equity. Equity has no equivalent shield: a dividend is paid out of profit that has already been taxed. So on a pure tax argument, debt is the cheaper funding and a company should use as much of it as it can — which is plainly wrong, and the reason it is wrong is the whole of capital structure theory. Beyond some level of borrowing, the costs of financial distress grow faster than the shield: lenders demand more, suppliers shorten terms, customers worry about warranties, management spends its time on the bank. The optimum is where the marginal value of the shield equals the marginal cost of distress, and no formula locates it.
The shield is worth nothing to a company with no taxable profit. A company making losses, or sheltered by large depreciation or tax credits, cannot use the deduction, so debt costs it the full 6 per cent. This is the standard reason a loss-making company leases rather than buys, and Chapter 11 develops it: the lessor has the taxable profit and can use the allowances, so it prices the lease to share some of the benefit.
Two further tax points belong here because questions pair them with the above. A premium or discount on issue is amortised for tax over the life of the bond rather than deducted at once. And in a refinancing, the unamortised issue costs of the old bond are normally deductible when it is retired, which improves the arithmetic of section 4.4 slightly.
Figure F604-06 collects the effects.
Figure F604-06. What the tax system does to each source of money
@table
Source | Deductible? | Before-tax cost | After-tax cost at 25%
Bank debt | Yes, the interest | 6.0% | 4.5%
Bond | Yes, the interest | 7.0% | 5.25%
Preferred stock | No, the dividend | 9.0% | 9.0%
Common equity | No, the dividend | 10.0% | 10.0%
@end
