Chapter summary
Figure F604-08 brings the chapter together.
Figure F604-08. Chapter 4 summary map: the key rule for each section.
A bond is worth the present value of what it promises: at a required 7 per cent, coupons of 60 × 4.1002 = 246.01 and a principal of 1,000 × 0.7130 = 713.00 give 246.01 + 713.00 = 959.01, and the same bond at 5 per cent is worth 1,043.27. A preferred share is a perpetuity, so 5.00 ÷ 0.08 = 62.50, and because no principal ever returns, its price carries the whole of any change in rates. Duration measures that sensitivity in one number: the five-year bond's cash flows average 4.45 years, modified duration is 4.45 ÷ 1.07 = 4.16, and a one-point rise in yields costs about 4.16 per cent of the price — a little less in truth, because convexity always favours the holder. From the issuer's side, a refinancing is a template: two points saved on ten million is 150,000 a year after tax against a call premium and issue costs of 375,000 after tax, so 375,000 ÷ 150,000 = 2.50 years of payback against five years of life, and the decision turns on the call provision the company paid for at issue. And through all of it runs the tax asymmetry: interest is deductible and dividends are not, so 6 per cent debt costs 4.5 after tax and Orontes's $390,000 of interest saves it $97,500 a year — until the costs of distress overtake the shield.
Practice
Answer the multiple-choice questions first. Then try the case-style set and the written task. The answers explain why each wrong option is wrong, so read them even when you are right.
