Case-style practice: Orontes considers a bond issue
Orontes needs $5,000,000 for a new roasting line. Its bank will lend at 6.5 per cent for five years with a covenant limiting debt to equity to 1.00. An investment bank proposes a public bond issue of the same amount at 7.0 per cent for ten years, with issue costs of $150,000 and no financial covenants. Orontes's effective tax rate is 25 per cent and its existing long-term debt is $6,000,000 against equity of $20,392,975.
Required. Compute the after-tax cost of each and say which you would recommend, naming one risk of each. Say also whether the covenant would bind. Under 250 words.
Answers and explanations are at the end of the chapter.
Answers and explanations
Section checks
SC4-1  Answer A. The two streams are discounted and added.
B is wrong. Par is what is repaid, not what the bond is worth today.
C is wrong. That is the annual coupon payment.
D is wrong. That is the current yield.
SC4-2  Answer A. 60 × 4.1002 = 246.01.
B is wrong. That is five coupons undiscounted.
C is wrong. That is one coupon.
D is wrong. 713.00 is the present value of the principal.
SC4-3  Answer A. Halve the payment and the rate, double the periods.
B is wrong. That is the annual treatment.
C is wrong. This doubles the periods without halving the payment.
D is wrong. This halves the payment without doubling the periods.
SC4-4  Answer A. 5.00 ÷ 0.08 = 62.50.
B is wrong. This divides by 0.125.
C is wrong. 50.00 is the value at a required 10 per cent.
D is wrong. This multiplies rather than divides.
SC4-5  Answer A. With no principal repayment, the price alone adjusts.
B is wrong. A preferred dividend is fixed.
C is wrong. Preferred stock ranks behind debt.
D is wrong. Being cumulative does not change rate sensitivity.
SC4-6  Answer A. Arrears must be cleared before any common dividend.
B is wrong. That describes non-cumulative preferred.
C is wrong. An undeclared dividend is not a liability.
D is wrong. Conversion requires a conversion right.
SC4-7  Answer A. Coupons return value early, pulling the average in.
B is wrong. Duration cannot exceed maturity for a straight bond.
C is wrong. They are equal only for a zero-coupon bond.
D is wrong. Duration is a time, not a rate.
SC4-8  Answer A. Modified duration is the percentage price change per point of yield.
B is wrong. That is the change in yield, not in price.
C is wrong. This divides rather than multiplies.
D is wrong. This misplaces the decimal.
SC4-9  Answer A. The longest maturity with no coupons has the highest duration.
B is wrong. Coupons pull the duration below ten years.
C is wrong. Five years is shorter than ten.
D is wrong. It is the shortest duration of the four.
SC4-10  Answer A. 375,000 ÷ 150,000 = 2.50 years.
B is wrong. This inverts the ratio.
C is wrong. Five years is the remaining life, not the payback.
D is wrong. This has no basis in the figures.
SC4-11  Answer A. Without a call provision the holders must agree.
B is wrong. Refinancing is attractive when rates have fallen, and still needs a call.
C is wrong. Security does not confer a right of early repayment.
D is wrong. Conversion is the holder's right, not the issuer's.
SC4-12  Answer A. 6.0 × 0.75 = 4.5 per cent.
B is wrong. That is the before-tax rate.
C is wrong. That is the shield alone.
D is wrong. Tax lowers the cost rather than raising it.
SC4-13  Answer A. A deduction is worth nothing without taxable profit to deduct it from.
B is wrong. A profitable company uses the shield fully.
C is wrong. A low rate makes the shield smaller, not worthless.
D is wrong. Interest is deductible.
SC4-14  Answer A. Distress costs eventually exceed the value of the shield.
B is wrong. Interest is deductible, which is why the argument starts.
C is wrong. Lenders stop lending, which is part of the distress.
D is wrong. Equity is normally the more expensive source.
