Multiple-choice questions
P4-01  Answer A. 50 × 3.4651 = 173.26 and 1,000 × 0.7921 = 792.10, which add to about 965.35.
B is wrong. The bond trades below par because its coupon is below the required return.
C is wrong. This is a premium, which would require rates below the coupon.
D is wrong. This values the principal and omits the coupons.
P4-02  Answer A. 4.00 ÷ 0.10 = 40.00.
B is wrong. That is one year's dividend.
C is wrong. This misplaces the decimal.
D is wrong. This divides by 0.01.
P4-03  Answer A. All the value arrives at maturity.
B is wrong. Halving applies to nothing here.
C is wrong. A zero-coupon bond is the most rate-sensitive of its maturity, not the least.
D is wrong. A zero-coupon bond has no coupon rate.
P4-04  Answer A. Divide by one plus the periodic required return.
B is wrong. The coupon rate is not the discount rate.
C is wrong. Maturity is an input to duration, not a divisor of it.
D is wrong. The price is already inside the weights.
P4-05  Answer A. A lower coupon returns less value early.
B is wrong. A higher coupon shortens duration.
C is wrong. A shorter maturity shortens duration.
D is wrong. A call provision shortens the expected life.
P4-06  Answer A. 200,000 × 0.75 = 150,000.
B is wrong. This ignores that the interest was deductible.
C is wrong. That is the tax effect alone.
D is wrong. Tax reduces the saving rather than increasing it.
P4-07  Answer A. 10,000,000 × 0.03 = 300,000.
B is wrong. This misplaces the decimal.
C is wrong. This is 30 per cent.
D is wrong. 103 is the call price per 100 of par.
P4-08  Answer A. 7.0 × 0.75 = 5.25 per cent.
B is wrong. That is the before-tax rate.
C is wrong. That is the shield alone.
D is wrong. This divides by 0.75 instead of multiplying.
P4-09  Answer A. A preferred dividend is paid out of taxed profit.
B is wrong. Loan interest is deductible.
C is wrong. Bond interest is deductible.
D is wrong. The interest element of a lease is deductible.
P4-10  Answer A. The price-yield relationship is curved in the holder's favour.
B is wrong. Duration is a straight-line approximation.
C is wrong. Duration remains the standard first-order measure.
D is wrong. Long bonds move more, not less.
Case-style set: Orontes considers a bond issue
The two costs. The bank loan at 6.5 per cent costs 6.5 × 0.75 = 4.875 per cent after tax. The bond at 7.0 per cent costs 7.0 × 0.75 = 5.25 per cent after tax, plus $150,000 of issue costs — which on $5,000,000 over ten years adds roughly a fifth of a point a year, taking the effective cost to about 5.45 per cent.
Whether the covenant binds. Debt to equity today is 6,000,000 ÷ 20,392,975 = 0.29 on long-term debt, or 11,279,625 ÷ 20,392,975 = 0.55 on total liabilities. Adding $5,000,000 of debt takes the second to 16,279,625 ÷ 20,392,975 = 0.80. On either measure the limit of 1.00 is not breached, so the covenant does not bind on these figures — though it would leave less room than Orontes has had, and Book 1 found a gross margin falling a point a year, which is how equity growth slows.
The recommendation. The bank loan, on cost and on simplicity. It is about half a point a year cheaper after tax, it carries no issue costs, and $5,000,000 is below the size at which a public issue's fixed costs are easily absorbed.
The risks. The loan's risk is the covenant: at 0.80 against a limit of 1.00 there is room, and a bad year that reduces equity narrows it, and Barada's experience in Book 1 shows what a breach costs. The bond's risk is the ten-year commitment at a fixed 7 per cent with no early repayment unless a call provision is bought — and if rates fall, Orontes would be paying above the market for several years with no way out.
